    "passage": "Mount Everest, the crown jewel of the Himalayas, is facing a new challenge: waste management. To safeguard the mountain’s environment, the authorities in Nepal have [[1]] a unique regulation—climbers must now pack out their own waste.\nDue to the extreme cold, human waste on Everest does not fully decompose, causing significant sanitation issues and [[2]]. Complaints have been received about visible human waste and climbers falling ill, prompting this initiative to protect the mountain’s environment and reputation.\nDuring the acclimatization period at base camp, climbers use designated toilet tents with barrels for waste collection. [[3]], in some locations higher up the mountain, snow is sparse and not enough to cover human waste. Thus, open defecation is common there. An estimated three tons of human waste are scattered between camp one and camp four, with South Col, the base before the final ascent, notoriously [[4]] as an “open toilet.”\nTo address this, climbers and their support staff are required to purchase specialized poo bags that contain chemicals and powders to solidify and deodorize the waste. Each climber, producing around 250 grams of excrement daily, [[5]] two bags a time, sufficient for their two-week stay at higher altitudes.\nThis pilot program is supported by environmental groups and the climbing community. It’s seen as a positive step towards cleaning up the Everest.",
